Mild Seronegative Rheumatoid Arthritis
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Morning stiffness lasting <30 minutes, responsive to basic NSAIDs, negative RF/CCP

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-031
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of symmetrical hand pain and immobility leading to extreme distress and fear of permanent deformity.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset, severe bilateral hand pain and stiffness. Immediate clinical differentiation from acute septic polyarthritis or a severe paraneoplastic process was required to manage profound acute pain and prevent unnecessary emergency department presentation prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My hands locked up completely tonight, the ache in my knuckles is excruciating, and I'm terrified I'm never going to be able to use them again.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports symmetrical small joint ache without markers, historically. Tonight, the patient experienced a sudden, severe flare of bilateral metacarpophalangeal (MCP) and proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joint pain following an unusually busy day of manual tasks. The pain is described as a deep, unrelenting ache that restricts hand closure. The acute functional loss has triggered severe anxiety regarding permanent crippling. Denies fever, recent local trauma, or prior diagnosis of crystal arthropathy.
Functional Status: Currently unable to perform fine motor tasks (e.g., turning doorknobs or opening bottles).
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone, acute pain limiting ability to care for self tonight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-oriented, visibly distressed and cradling both hands.
Physical Exam: Bilateral hands demonstrate mild to moderate swelling over the 2nd and 3rd MCP and PIP joints. Mild diffuse tenderness to palpation, but strictly no erythema, exquisite heat, or fluctuance to suggest acute infection. Active range of motion is limited by pain, but passive range of motion is intact. No tenosynovitis or carpal tunnel signs. History confirms morning stiffness lasting <30 minutes, responsive to basic NSAIDs, negative RF/CCP.
Environmental Observations: Safe, warm environment, no immediate hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient uses over-the-counter paracetamol sparingly.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute mechanical overuse flare in Mild Seronegative Rheumatoid Arthritis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile presentation, bilateral/symmetrical involvement, and lack of severe, red, hot, swollen joints with extreme pain on passive micro-motion.

Acute Viral Polyarthropathy (e.g., Parvovirus) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of preceding viral prodrome, rash, or widespread systemic symptoms.

Crystal Arthropathy (Gout/Pseudogout) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symmetrical, multi-joint presentation rather than a classic severe monoarthritis, and lack of tophi.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to acute functional impairment tonight)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe bilateral hand flare, mimicking an aggressive inflammatory or potentially infectious process. Triage appropriately escalated the case to rule out septic polyarthritis and manage acute functional loss. Clinical bedside evaluation confirmed symmetrical, non-erythematous joint swelling consistent with a mechanical/overuse exacerbation of a known, mild seronegative inflammatory arthritis. The rapid provision of targeted anti-inflammatory therapy and clinical reassurance was critical to de-escalate the patient's panic over permanent deformity and restore basic hand function, safely preventing an inappropriate emergency department visit tonight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral NSAIDs (ibuprofen 400mg) and demonstrated the use of warm water therapy.
Positive Behavioral Change: Submerge both hands in a bowl of warm water and gently open and close your fists for 5 minutes, followed immediately by taking the prescribed anti-inflammatory with food. The heat will rapidly increase local blood flow and wash away inflammatory chemicals, drastically reducing stiffness and allowing the medication to work faster.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 7 days to discuss formal rheumatology referral and long-term NSAID/DMARD management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if a single joint suddenly becomes intensely red, hot to the touch, and severely painful to even move slightly, or if you develop a high fever with chills.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This type of arthritis causes inflammation but very rarely causes the severe, rapid joint destruction seen in classic Rheumatoid Arthritis. Flares are typically short-lived and responsive to basic medications.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the pain spreads to large joints like your knees or shoulders, or if the swelling persists unchanged for more than a week despite taking NSAIDs.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your joints are aching because the lining is temporarily inflamed due to overuse, not because the bones are crumbling. The anti-inflammatory medication will physically shrink the swelling inside the joint.
Actionable Steps: Rest your hands completely tomorrow. Use warm water soaks in the morning. Follow up with your GP to get a long-term management plan.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Flare of mild inflammatory arthritis. No signs of joint infection or rapid damage. Use warm water soaks and prescribed anti-inflammatories. See GP in 7 days.

SMS EDUCATION

Soak hands in warm water to relieve stiffness. Take NSAIDs with food. Go to the ED if a single joint becomes bright red, hot, or you get a high fever.


